<h2>The case in brief</h2>
<p>The Anti-Corruption Bureau, Jaipur registered FIR No. 245/2024 alleging a scheme in the tendering of Jal Jeevan Mission works in Rajasthan&rsquo;s Public Health Engineering Department. According to the prosecution, two contractors deployed forged work-experience certificates to win approximately 104 tenders valued at about Rs 979.45 crore, and officials sitting on the evaluation and finance committees smoothed the path. The appellants before the Supreme Court &mdash; Subodh Agarwal, with connected petitions by Dinesh Goel and Mahendra Prakash Soni &mdash; were among the senior officials arrayed as facilitators.</p>
<p>The Rajasthan High Court declined bail to Agarwal and Soni on 13 August 2026 and to Goel on 23 September 2026. The appeals, arising out of SLP (Crl.) Nos. 19042, 19335 and 19254 of 2026, were decided together on 25 November 2026.</p>

<h2>What tipped the balance</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Probe complete.</strong> The investigation qua the appellants was over and chargesheets had been filed, so arrest-stage considerations such as custodial interrogation had exhausted themselves.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A trial years away.</strong> The record runs to thousands of pages and the prosecution cites 124 witnesses. Charges had not even been framed. The Court saw no realistic early conclusion.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Parity.</strong> Several co-accused &mdash; including the former PHED minister &mdash; had already been enlarged on bail, leaving the appellants&rsquo; continued custody without a distinguishing rationale.</div>
</div>

<h2>The principle restated</h2>
<p>The Bench put the matter in terms familiar from the Court&rsquo;s recent bail jurisprudence: detention before conviction is a means of securing the trial, not an advance instalment of sentence. Where the State has finished investigating and the trial cannot begin in earnest for a long time, the seriousness of the accusation &mdash; even one involving public money on this scale &mdash; does not by itself justify keeping an accused inside. The Court was careful to say it expressed no view on the merits, and it nudged the trial court to frame charges expeditiously so that the case moves.</p>
<p>Notably, this is the second bail ruling from the same investigation to reach the Supreme Court in recent weeks; an earlier order concerning other accused in the Jal Jeevan tender matter proceeded on similar lines. The consistent thread is that economic-offence gravity is weighed, not worshipped.</p>

<h2>Why it matters in practice</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Stage of case</th><th>Bail consequence after this ruling</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Chargesheet filed, charges not framed</td><td>Strong ground to argue custody has no investigative purpose; delay in framing cuts in favour of the accused</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Voluminous documentary trial</td><td>Length of the prospective trial is a quantifiable factor &mdash; witness counts and record size belong in the bail petition</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Co-accused on bail</td><td>Parity remains a live argument where roles are comparable, including for public servants vis-&agrave;-vis private contractors</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Post-bail conduct</td><td>Regular attendance is the price of liberty; absence invites cancellation</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">This report summarises a judgment of the Supreme Court of India dated 25 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice, and it expresses no view on the pending trial.</div>
